From: deepseek-v4-cmp170hx
Subject: [PATCH 0022] scheduler: add a template-correct "reopen turn"
 recovery mode alongside 0021's freeform nudge

WHAT THIS FIXES. Live testing of `0021` past ~100k context found the
nudge splice fires as designed (confirmed via logs: "spliced a recovery
nudge (attempt 1/2)", then "attempt 2/2") but the model keeps mutating
into new short repeats even after taking the nudge ("Let me check" ->
"Check the run" -> "Check."), burning the whole `DSV4_NUDGE_MAX_RETRIES`
budget without actually recovering -- worse than the ~100k-and-under
case this mechanism was validated against.

Two independent findings point at the same suspect. First,
ggml-org/llama.cpp#26694 reports the identical shape -- repetition loops
in long agentic chats (large system prompt + tool schemas + format-heavy
markup) that reproduce locally but not against the official DeepSeek
API, with thinking-mode toggling and temperature ruled out; the reporter
suspects "chat-template / stop-token handling" losing track of turn
boundaries deep in an extended agentic context. Second, `0021`'s own
nudge is freeform text spliced mid-turn with NO template boundary at
all -- it's appended into the same raw token stream the loop was already
degenerating in, so if the boundary-tracking state itself is what's
corrupted, a plain-text nudge never touches the actual problem.

THE MECHANISM. `_dsv4_reopen_turn()` splices the exact turn-boundary
token sequence a genuine new `/v1/chat/completions` call would produce
for a fresh user turn saying "go", instead of freeform text: close the
current (truncated) assistant turn with the eos token, open a user turn,
open the next assistant turn. This is not a guess at the format -- it's
read directly from vllm/tokenizers/deepseek_v4_encoding.py:
`assistant_msg_template = "{reasoning}{content}{tool_calls}" + eos_token`
followed immediately by `USER_SP_TOKEN` / user content / `ASSISTANT_SP_TOKEN`
in `render_message()`'s user-role branch. Splicing this (rather than
literally ending the stream and letting the client re-open a new HTTP
request) keeps the recovery server-side and same-turn like `0021`, but
gives the model a real, unambiguous, freshly-tokenized turn boundary
to reset against instead of asking it to recover from inside the same
corrupted-boundary context.

Same non-advancing-num_computed_tokens splice shape as `0021`'s
`_dsv4_splice_nudge()` -- see that function's docstring for why forcing
a real forward pass over the spliced tokens (rather than treating them
as already-computed) is safe. Shares `_dsv4_nudge_count` /
`DSV4_NUDGE_MAX_RETRIES` with the nudge path -- the two strategies are
mutually exclusive per request, chosen once via `DSV4_RECOVERY_MODE`
(default "nudge", opt-in "reopen"), never mixed mid-request.

`_dsv4_recover()` is a thin dispatcher: both tripwire call sites in
`0020`/`0021` now go through it instead of calling `_dsv4_splice_nudge()`
directly, so switching strategies is a single env var
(`DSV4_RECOVERY_MODE=reopen`), not a code change. `DSV4_NUDGE=0` still
disables recovery entirely regardless of mode, same as before.

STATUS: untested. This is the "b" experiment in a planned A/B --
`top_p=0.95` + `--reasoning-parser deepseek_v4` is being tried first
against the same >100k-context repro; this patch is staged so the
reopen-mode retest can follow immediately without a rebuild delay if
the sampling-side fix alone doesn't hold.

Touches `vllm/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py` only, on top of `0021` in the
same file. Applies after `0021` in glob order.
